<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Alcoraz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1096">1096</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Alcoraz was fought in November 1096 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, where Peter I of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> defeated the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, which had Castilian support. Huesca surrendered shortly afterwards and became a royal seat of the kingdom. A later legend that Saint George fought for the Aragonese gave rise to the Cross of Alcoraz with four Moors' heads in the arms of Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcoraz.jpg" alt="Battle of Alcoraz" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeronimo Martínez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creu-Alcoraz-1524-Jeronimo-Martinez-Retablo-San-Jorge.Salvador-Merce-Teruel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was killed besieging Huesca, which his son Peter I took after the <b>Battle of Alcoraz</b> in 1096; Barbastro followed in 1100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
